E5 uses 69.31\% fewer LUTs and 44.30\% fewer FFs than E1 at equal one-DSP budget and the same full input domain. Admission and correction are included. E8 uses 47 LUTs, 36 FFs and one DSP; the combined E5 admission/full-width-support cost is therefore 15 LUTs and eight FFs at the same DSP count. All four explicit narrow-domain pair/scalar unstalled/stalled tests passed an independent output, source and route audit. This does not imply universal FPGA area superiority: E4 is smaller but produces one result per issue, and E3 uses fewer LUTs and provides two independent results with two DSPs. All-wide bursts with matching consecutive coefficients favor E1; mismatched coefficients force singletons in both. We do not claim universal burst-latency improvement.

The original E2 fixed-sixteen-bit route hit a Vivado \texttt{Synth 20-411} error after completed synthesis. A source-identical checkpoint retry passed with 340 LUTs, 88 FFs and zero DSPs. Its GLIF and OTTT stress delayed replays both failed despite successful SDF annotation; their energy is rejected. E7 independently passed general unstalled/stalled native checks, the actual GLIF native capture, its matched 12 ns route at 330 LUTs, 88 FFs and zero DSPs, and representative GLIF delayed replay and energy. Historical signed-twelve-bit E2 passed functional replay and static timing but failed delayed replay at rows 914 and 2836. The original low-lane failure is 509 instead of $-3$ for $(U,\beta)=(-6,166)$; the other lane and control fields match.

Token-aware inspection establishes three missing EOF closing parentheses in vendor SDF exports. Certified derivatives retain every original byte, numerical delay, path and timing check and append only those structural closes; netlists change only their quoted SDF filename. A matched raw-versus-balanced E2 control still fails identically at row 914, ruling out that syntax repair as the numerical remedy. In an independently annotated combined shadow test, the original CARRY4 output remains wrong after more than 6 ns of stable observed inputs while its shadow is correct; the maximum annotated path is 579 ps. Disabling simulator optimization also retains row 914. Suppressing the connected carry-input pulse clears that first error under identical observed select/data sequences, but replay fails later at row 2836. Because forcing can affect connected nets, this is a diagnostic intervention, not pin-local causal isolation or a valid implementation repair. Changing all pulse limits from zero to 100\% clears row 914 but fails at row 5701; changing only error limits produces an earlier unknown-valued result and is rejected.

An observation-only probe of the unchanged certified signed-sixteen-bit OTTT E2 export reproduces row 2175. Subsequently, its carry cell retains output value 2 with zero select/data/carry inputs and zero carry feedback after more than 19 ns of stable inputs, contradicting its combinational truth relation. This extends the settled carry-cell symptom to the new width. The exact event-scheduling cause and a physical hardware defect remain unproved; neither intervention supplies accepted full replay or energy. E7's acceptance cannot retroactively resolve E2's diagnostic.

Earlier twelve-bit values of 53 versus 122 LUTs and 34.4\% lower energy were from a separate 17,408-operand prefix sample. Its coefficient distribution did not pass the later representative audit. Those results remain historical implementation/sample evidence and are not substituted for the new fixed-sixteen-bit measurements.

\section{Related work and applicability limits}
FireFly~\cite{firefly} uses fixed twelve-bit SIMD additions and spike-controlled multiplexers; its original $\tau=2$ leakage is shift-based. Our fixed split and LUT controls are built for the same leakage primitive, not reproduced FireFly accelerators. Its whole-accelerator throughput or accuracy cannot be compared directly with kernel issues or fixture energy. Testing public LIF and GLIF checkpoints supports applicability but is not reproduction of FireFly's trained SCNN models.

The inspected DSP-Packing, Arithmetic Packing, XtraMAC and Brooks--Martonosi provide substantial ancestry. UDP's complete methods~\cite{udp} select packing parallelism from specified operand widths and generate parameterized hardware. Its runtime sign and most-negative-value corrections preserve exact arithmetic; they do not describe our actual-membrane pair/singleton admission rule. Its high/low fusion also supplies ancestry for splitting. Full methods for three relevant originals remain unread: Ou's dynamic DSP packing~\cite{ou}, Cui's six-signed multiplication~\cite{cui}, and McKeown's dynamic-range utilization~\cite{mckeown}. Publisher access gates and author/institutional routes have not supplied those complete methods. The bounded review has not identified this exact implemented leakage/admission conjunction, but it does not establish absence or priority. The same stored-state policy must remain compared with strong fixed and fabric alternatives. Learned GLIF coefficients are fixed per channel except for previous-spike selection between two values; a specialized constant multiplier bank or CSD implementation is not measured. General runtime-coefficient comparisons therefore do not establish superiority over every model-specific implementation.

\section{Conclusion}
The exact membrane-range-selected kernel retains fixed sixteen-bit precision and provides measured alternatives at a one-DSP budget: lower fabric cost than fixed split packing, and fewer GLIF leakage issues and lower estimated dynamic energy than scalar execution. A two-DSP scalar pair has near energy parity and fewer issues, so adaptation offers a DSP-budget trade-off. The narrow-only ablation measures a combined 15-LUT/eight-FF cost for range admission and wide support. The complete vanilla LIF test and bounded thirty-step BNTT test confirm exact packed leakage and the fixed storage domain, while demonstrating unfavorable range profiles and essential zero-DSP constant alternatives; the measured half-leakage shift consumes less energy than adaptation. All planned controls were attempted and failed LUT evidence is excluded. These results support a focused exact leakage primitive paper, with model-specific constant hardware, external source integration, access-limited prior methods and the original LUT delayed-replay cause retained as applicability and evidence limits.

